finding.evidence:
In the audited source (thin-vec 0.2.15), clear() at lines 1082-1087 is:
    pub fn clear(&mut self) {
        unsafe {
            ptr::drop_in_place(&mut self[..]);
            self.set_len(0); // could be the singleton
        }
    }
Input/flow: an attacker (or any safe caller) builds a `<ThinVec<T>>` whose element type T has a user-controlled/attacker-influenced `Drop` impl that can panic (line 1084 drops every element in `self[..]`). `drop_in_place` runs the elements' destructors sequentially; if any destructor panics, unwinding starts from inside `drop_in_place` and the following line `self.set_len(0)` (line 1085) is skipped. ThinVec's own length therefore still reports the original `len` even though the buffer's elements were (partially) destroyed. The buffer contents are now logically uninitialized but the vector still claims ownership of `len` live elements. Unsafe operation: on any later `drop`/`truncate`/`clear` of that same vector, `drop_in_place(&mut this[..])` (ThinVec::drop, line 1917) drops the already-dropped range again, producing a double drop, which for owning element types (e.g. `Box<T>`, `String`, `ThinVec`, `Rc`) is a double free; for interior-ref types it is a use-after-free. Failed protections: this version has NO panic guard around the drop; the correctness of `set_len(0)` depends on `drop_in_place` returning normally, which is not guaranteed for safe types with panicking `Drop`. The vulnerability is confirmed by the upstream fix: every later release (0.2.16/0.2.17/0.2.18 present on this system) replaces these two lines with a `DropGuard` that calls `self.0.set_len(0)` in its `Drop`, i.e. it resets the length even on unwind; the 0.2.16 tree also adds `test_panic_clear` asserting the panic path. Required configuration: none beyond the default crate build; any successful panic escaping an element destructor during `clear()` on a non-empty `ThinVec<T>` where T owns heap memory reaches the unsafe double-drop.

The IntoIter destructor's cold path (lines 2542-2550) is:
    fn drop_non_singleton<T>(this: &mut IntoIter<T>) {
        unsafe {
            let mut vec = mem::replace(&mut this.vec, ThinVec::new());
            ptr::drop_in_place(&mut vec[this.start..]);
            vec.set_len_non_singleton(0)
        }
    }
Input/flow: an attacker creates a `ThinVec<T>` whose element `Drop` can panic, moves it into an `IntoIter`, and consumes some prefix with `next()` (this advances `self.start` and `ptr::read`s those elements out, so elements `[0, start)` are already logically moved-out but physically still in the buffer). When the `IntoIter` is dropped, line 2546 `mem::replace` moves the whole vector into the local `vec` (with `len` still counting the consumed prefix), line 2547 drops only `vec[start..]`, and line 2548 is supposed to zero the length so the local `vec` does not re-drop the consumed prefix. Unsafe operation: if any destructor in `vec[start..]` panics, unwinding leaves `vec` (a live `ThinVec<T>` owning `len` elements) to be dropped by its own `Drop` impl, which drops `vec[..]` including the already-consumed `[0, start)` elements, double-dropping them. Failed protections: line 2548 runs only if `drop_in_place` returns normally; the consumed-prefix elements are protected solely by a post-drop length reset, which unwinding bypasses. The upstream fix is visible on this system: 0.2.16/0.2.17/0.2.18 replace this body with a `DropGuard` whose `Drop` calls `self.0.vec.set_len_non_singleton(0)`, and add `test_panic_into_iter`. Required configuration: default build; the sink is reachable from safe code whenever a `T: Drop` panics while an `IntoIter` over a `ThinVec<T>` (with at least one consumed prefix element and one owning element) is dropped.
